Reason:
The probe function does not create a devlink between the backlight consumer (`&pdev->dev`) and the actual LED class device’s supplier parent (`priv->leds[i]->dev->parent`). Without this link, the kernel’s default devlink mechanism incorrectly associates the consumer with the parent of the LED class device (e.g., the I2C bus adapter). This causes an incorrect removal order: the LED chip driver can be unbound before the backlight driver, leading to a NULL pointer dereference when the backlight tries to access the LED during cleanup.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
for (i = 0; i < priv->nb_leds; i++) {
	struct device_link *link;

	link = device_link_add(&pdev->dev, priv->leds[i]->dev->parent,
			       DL_FLAG_AUTOREMOVE_CONSUMER);
	if (!link) {
		dev_err(&pdev->dev, "Failed to add devlink (consumer %s, supplier %s)\n",
			dev_name(&pdev->dev), dev_name(priv->leds[i]->dev->parent));
		backlight_device_unregister(priv->bl_dev);
		return -EINVAL;
	}
}
```
Reason:
This loop creates a proper device link from the backlight device (consumer) to the LED chip device (supplier) for each LED. The supplier is `priv->leds[i]->dev->parent`, which is the actual LED driver chip (e.g., the I2C device) rather than its parent bus adapter. The `DL_FLAG_AUTOREMOVE_CONSUMER` flag ensures the link is automatically removed when the consumer is removed, enforcing correct removal order: the backlight device will be unbound before its supplier LED chip driver. This prevents the NULL pointer dereference and resolves the improper ordering issue.

